        <p class="lead">
          You try to open an iPhone photo on your Windows PC and get an error. The file has a .HEIC extension. What is HEIC, why does Apple use it, and how do you deal with it?
        </p>

        <h2>What is HEIC?</h2>
        <p>HEIC stands for <strong>High Efficiency Image Container</strong>. It's Apple's implementation of the HEIF (High Efficiency Image Format) standard. The format uses the same compression technology as HEVC video (also known as H.265), which is significantly more efficient than the older JPG standard.</p>
        <p>In practical terms, a HEIC photo is roughly the same visual quality as a JPG but about <strong>50% smaller in file size</strong>. For a phone that takes 48-megapixel photos, that storage savings adds up fast.</p>

        <h2>Why Apple Uses HEIC</h2>
        <p>Apple switched from JPG to HEIC as the default photo format starting with iOS 11 in 2017. The reasons are straightforward:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Storage efficiency</strong> — HEIC files are half the size of JPG, meaning more photos fit on a device</li>
          <li><strong>Higher quality at smaller sizes</strong> — The compression algorithm is more sophisticated than JPG's 1990s-era technology</li>
          <li><strong>Feature support</strong> — HEIC can store multiple images (like Live Photos), depth maps for Portrait mode, and HDR data</li>
          <li><strong>Battery efficiency</strong> — Smaller files mean less processing power and battery used for saving and reading photos</li>
          <li><strong>10-bit color</strong> — HEIC supports wider color gamuts that modern iPhone cameras capture</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The Compatibility Problem</h2>
        <p>HEIC is technically superior to JPG, but it has one major weakness: <strong>not everyone can open it</strong>. Here's where HEIC doesn't work:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Windows PCs (older versions)</strong> — Windows 10 before version 1803 cannot open HEIC natively</li>
          <li><strong>Android devices</strong> — Most Android phones can't open HEIC without third-party apps</li>
          <li><strong>Web browsers</strong> — Most browsers don't support HEIC display</li>
          <li><strong>Social media uploads</strong> — Some platforms reject HEIC files</li>
          <li><strong>Email attachments</strong> — Recipients may not be able to view HEIC photos</li>
          <li><strong>Printing services</strong> — Most print shops and online services don't accept HEIC</li>
          <li><strong>Photo editing software</strong> — Older versions of Photoshop, Lightroom, and other editors don't support HEIC</li>
          <li><strong>Government portals</strong> — Visa applications, passport systems, and other official forms require JPG or PNG</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How to Convert HEIC to JPG</h2>
        <p>Converting HEIC to JPG is the simplest solution. Here are the main methods:</p>

        <h3>Online Converters (Recommended)</h3>
        <p>Our <a href="/heic-to-jpg">HEIC to JPG converter</a> processes images entirely in your browser. No files are uploaded to any server. Simply drag and drop your HEIC photos and download JPG versions instantly.</p>

        <h3>On Mac</h3>
        <p>If you have a Mac, you can open HEIC photos in Preview and export them as JPG. Go to File → Export → Choose JPEG from the format dropdown. This works but is slow for large batches of photos.</p>

        <h3>On iPhone</h3>
        <p>You can set your iPhone to save photos as JPG instead of HEIC. Go to Settings → Camera → Formats → Most Compatible. Note that this uses more storage space on your device.</p>

